#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build a liturgical slice's act-keyed history as a Git repository.

Usage: act-history {check,graph,emit} [--source PATH] [--out DIR] [--json]

The station is the act, never the book. Every commit is a promulgation, a
revision, a reform, a rubrical code or a documented exemption, and carries the
instrument it cites; a printing is a witness and hangs beneath the history in
`witnesses/`. That is what keeps a scanner out of the record: two printings of
one prayer differ constantly and no act stands behind those differences, so none
of them can become a commit.

Three things this does that a linear generator cannot, and the third is the one
the shape exists for:

  MULTIPLE PARENTS   an act may apply a decree to a text, and both are parents.
                     A merge is a claim that a reception happened, so `check`
                     refuses any act with two parents that does not cite one.
  MULTIPLE BRANCHES  one ref per line. Lines never rejoin: no act may name
                     parents on two different lines, and nothing here can draw
                     a merge across them.
  PARALLEL STAYS PARALLEL  a fork is born at an act. Where a line's other side
                     has no witness in the corpus, the branch is a root and
                     nothing more, and its emptiness is the record.

Commit dates are the acts' own dates, shifted forward ten thousand years. That
is not cosmetic and not inherited thoughtlessly: git 2.55 rejects a negative
timestamp outright -- `git fsck --strict` reports `badDate` and every date
format prints empty -- so a true 1570 date cannot be stored in a way any tool
will show. A shift small enough to look plausible would be read as a real date;
11570 cannot be mistaken for anything. The true date is in the tree, in the
commit message, and in an `Act-date` trailer, so nothing depends on the stamp.
"""
